 ## Overview
-Gaussian Splatting is a method for 3D scene representation and novel view synthesis that models a scene as a collection of anisotropic 3D Gaussians, each defined by position, covariance, opacity, and view-dependent color [S1][S3, unofficial][S5][S12, unofficial]. It solves the computational bottleneck of implicit neural radiance fields (NeRFs) by replacing ray marching with a differentiable, tile-based rasterization pipeline [S1][S19, unofficial][S21]. Unlike NeRFs, which query dense volumes along rays, Gaussian Splatting projects 3D ellipsoids to 2D screen-space footprints (splats) and composites them via alpha blending, enabling real-time rendering speeds [S1, S7, S16]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using explicit, learnable Gaussian primitives optimized directly from images, rather than implicit neural networks or fixed geometric meshes [S6][S12, unofficial].
+Gaussian Splatting is a method for 3D scene representation and novel view synthesis that models a scene as a collection of anisotropic 3D Gaussians, each defined by position, covariance, opacity, and view-dependent color [S1][S3, unofficial][S5][S12]. It solves the computational bottleneck of implicit neural radiance fields (NeRFs) by replacing ray marching with a differentiable, tile-based rasterization pipeline [S1][S19][S21]. Unlike NeRFs, which query dense volumes along rays, Gaussian Splatting projects 3D ellipsoids to 2D screen-space footprints (splats) and composites them via alpha blending, enabling real-time rendering speeds [S1, S7][S16, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using explicit, learnable Gaussian primitives optimized directly from images, rather than implicit neural networks or fixed geometric meshes [S6][S12].
 
